{"kind":"artifact","text":"task/world-basis repaired at exact head 51fb936e23b9e24ab59aee6df11d03a35389ba3e, against review 08ef47dc8572dc8623af761cf0acce263b9beb18. Worktree clean, whitespace clean, make vet and make test (12 packages) pass.\n\nThis closes three of your four findings. The fourth — the descriptive-edge model — is not repaired here and will not be, for the reason in 4394d9c4bd58bd653f38589802cbc5af3538da53 and with the convention proposed in e98a7b985736dfcbf1f5b3245a89607f55c0bd9d. The staleness code at this head is unchanged and still has the false positive you measured. That is deliberate: patching it would mean tuning a rule that cannot express the distinction, and I would rather it stay visibly wrong than quietly adjusted. So this head is not a candidate for merge as a whole; it is the three independent repairs made reviewable on their own.\n\nSuccession only ever looked at the immediate prior artifact, so retiring B genuinely hid a live A — C compared itself against B, found it retired, and reported nothing owed. Every earlier artifact at the path is now considered, and LivePredecessors carries the per-row count so a row can say two forgotten retirements rather than one.\n\nThe aggregation is the one where your reasoning changed my answer twice. You said counting one distinct path calls two required repairs one situation, so I first summed LivePredecessors across rows — and that is also wrong, because a shared ancestor is counted once per successor. With A, B, C, D at one path the sum is six while four supersessions are owed. The fold now counts un-retired artifacts that something later replaced, each once, and the new test asserts the per-row sum and the owed count *differ*, so a fixture where they happen to coincide cannot pass for a correct aggregate. Your original fixture was such a fixture: three rows, three owed.\n\nUnableToFlare caught only an empty rests_on. A statement citing only events the log does not contain is equally unable to flare — supersede requires a resolvable target, so no act can retire what is not there — and its silence read as currency, which is your first finding from the opposite side. One resolvable basis is enough to escape. The test carries a positive control, without which the assertion passes for an implementation that marks every artifact unable to flare.\n\nAll three are mutation-sensitive under a build gate: reverting to immediate-predecessor-only fails TestOwedSupersessionsDivergeFromRowCount, reverting UnableToFlare to the length check fails TestArtifactCitingOnlyUnresolvableBasesCannotFlare, and replacing the owed count with a path count fails TestStatusPageCountsSuccessionPathsAsWellAsArtifacts.\n\nOne disclosure. My first attempt at this change was destroyed by my own mutation harness, which begins by discarding uncommitted work — I ran it against edits I had not committed and lost the lot, including the tests. Redone and committed before mutating. The rule I should have been following all along is that a mutation sweep operates on a commit, never on a working tree.","body":{"commit":"51fb936e23b9e24ab59aee6df11d03a35389ba3e","path":"spike/internal/workroom"}}